I picked up Her Story on sale looking for a
game and after I had noticed how successful it was. The concept was alluring: searching keywords on an in-game database to uncover videos of an interrogation, and consequently more keywords, to ultimately
the case. Alas, I'm hard-pressed to say that there's no actual crime
in this game. The idea is to have you, the player, stumbling across a sea of small clips in hopes of uncovering what went on in this case, for your own personal awareness. The case itself, as you'll see towards the end, was solved before you "got there". For what I was looking for, this doesn't hinder the overall quality of the game, just letting whom it may concern know that
. They place this game in the
genre for good reason. With this out of the way, considering the way the game is structured - where your perception of the flow of the game/plot seems somewhat dependent on which keywords you type first and which videos you see first - I feel that I've come across key information at what I would call the right time. This made uncovering the story an exciting experience, with a handful of twists and turns that made Her Story worth my undivided attention for around 4 hours (I even went online a bit looking for theories after I was done with the game). I got the end credits a bit earlier than I should have, and proceeded to get to the remaining 15-20 clips using a code (you can find it anywhere online if you miss the in-game tip). Once I had everything, went through a few clips once again to seek closure. It was fun while it lasted.
I'm a firm believer that this concept has untapped potential. Her Story comes close to fully materializing this potential, but falls short in its presentation, overall scope and extras... which are next to nonexistent. With all the care put into the plot and making the game feel like something straight out of the mid 90's, you'd expect a user interface that actually makes sense. The most prominent example of this fault comes with how you're given the option to keep some clips aside on this "user session" tab, but once you've sent them there you can't remove them, something that makes organizing your clues quite harder, if not done from memory. I would have also liked to have a little more to tinker with. There's a minigame very similar to Reversi that serves no interest other than getting an achievement. Once you're done with the game's short but beautiful, cryptic plot, there isn't much else to do and virtually no reason to come back.
Full price or on sale, I recommend this game to every fan of drama/crime novels, movies, series, games, etc., above all.
6.5/10
